Offscreen multisampled render targets through EXT_multisampled_render_to_texture (MSRTT) where the driver has it.
bgfx used the extension only for the multisampled back buffer; an offscreen MSAA target (the scene, the UI layers)
was a multisample renderbuffer in memory plus a glBlitFramebuffer resolve into the texture: a tile GPU wrote every
sample to memory and read it back for the blit. With MSRTT the texture itself is attached with
glFramebufferTexture2DMultisampleEXT: the samples stay in tile memory and only resolved pixels are written.
- A 2D MSAA target that MSRTT can serve (colour, or a write-only depth/stencil) gets its multisample storage with
  its framebuffer; the framebuffer takes MSRTT when every multisampled attachment can (a sampled multisampled depth
  texture, the scene on «Высокая» with depth effects, cannot: that framebuffer keeps renderbuffers and the blit,
  logged). Write-only depth/stencil: a glRenderbufferStorageMultisampleEXT renderbuffer.
- The path is logged once at init ("BGFX offscreen MSAA: ..."); without the extension nothing changes.
- Discarding an MSRTT framebuffer's colour would invalidate the resolved texture: only depth/stencil are discarded.
